{
  "id": "ella-fitzgerald-ella-fitzgerald-sings-the-cole-porter-songbook-1956",
  "artist": "Ella Fitzgerald",
  "album": "Ella Fitzgerald Sings the Cole Porter Song Book",
  "year": 1956,
  "label": "Verve",
  "style_primary": "cool-jazz",
  "rationale": "The record that invented the American Songbook album and made Verve a label: Ella Fitzgerald's first release for Norman Granz's new imprint, 32 Cole Porter songs arranged and conducted by Buddy Bregman, recorded in Hollywood in February and March 1956. It is the founding entry of the eight-album Songbook series and the one that changed Fitzgerald's standing from admired jazz singer to the First Lady of Song. Institutional consensus is documented and independent of one another: Grammy Hall of Fame (2000), Library of Congress National Recording Registry (2003, one of 50 recordings chosen that year), Penguin Guide to Jazz Recordings 4/4, The Encyclopedia of Popular Music 5/5, AllMusic 4.5/5, Rolling Stone Jazz Record Guide 4/5. Lineage is equally concrete — Ira Gershwin's often-quoted line that he never knew how good the songs were until he heard Ella sing them, and the songbook format that every subsequent standards album in this canon (Lady in Satin, the Getz and Evans songbook records) assumes. The canon currently holds exactly one Fitzgerald album (Ella and Louis, 1956) and none of the Songbooks; this is the largest hole in its vocal wing. Session gravity is lighter than a small-group date — the interest is Bregman's orchestra and the West Coast studio players inside it (Bud Shank, Herb Geller, Bob Cooper, Barney Kessel, Alvin Stoller) — but the record is a defining statement on its own terms, not a supporting document.",
  "priority": "strong",
  "epistemic": "obs",
  "include": null,
  "scope_flag": "Inside the 1945-1985 window and inside the canon's vocal wing. The honest caveat for the council: this is a big-band/traditional-pop songbook rather than a small-group post-bebop date — Granz said outright that the first Verve album would not be a jazz project, but rather a songbook. Its claim is canon-defining status and lineage, not hard-bop session gravity.",
