  "ballot": {
    "album": "Ella Fitzgerald — Ella Fitzgerald Sings the Cole Porter Song Book (1956)",
    "scope_check": {
      "in_window": true,
      "boundary_note": "Inside the year window with no excluded-style gate; the disputed boundary is orchestral vocal jazz/traditional pop versus the canon's post-bebop center, not chronological eligibility. Both references question the dossier's cool-jazz classification."
    },
    "tier": "scope_call",
    "priority": "strong",
    "case_for": "This is a defining Fitzgerald statement: Verve's inaugural release and the opening installment of her Songbook cycle, making sustained interpretation of a composer's work a major jazz-album achievement (S1, S3, S6). Grammy Hall of Fame recognition, National Recording Registry selection, and strong ratings across several critical guides constitute an exceptional consensus signal, although the dossier reports these through S1 rather than direct institutional citations. Fitzgerald's swing, phrasing, and lyrical clarity make Porter's repertoire an inviting discovery route into jazz standards, with substantial supporting jazz personnel including Barney Kessel, Bud Shank, and Harry Edison (S1–S2).",
    "case_against": "Its stature as American vocal music does not automatically establish that it is load-bearing within a post-bebop discovery canon: Bregman's arranged orchestral settings foreground songs and polished interpretation rather than improvisational exchange. The strongest aesthetic objection is that this presentation teaches the prestige songbook tradition more directly than the musical developments at the canon's center. The evidence also needs tightening: institutional honors and guide ratings are mediated through Wikipedia, while claims that this album invented the format or directly underlies virtually every later standards project exceed what the supplied sources establish.",
    "disagreement": "Reference 1 assigns consensus_core/must_have, treating the established vocal wing and Fitzgerald's jazz identity as sufficient to resolve the stylistic caveat; Reference 2 assigns scope_call/strong, treating the orchestral traditional-pop presentation as a substantive boundary requiring John's ruling. Both recognize the album's landmark stature and the sourcing and classification weaknesses; they disagree about whether that stature settles its fit within this particular canon.",
    "recommendation": "The council proposes that John consider this a strong candidate and explicitly rule whether its defining orchestral Songbook achievement belongs within the canon's vocal-jazz scope.",
